[{:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-11-07T11:48:47.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-11-07T11:48:47.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://jmglov.net/blog/2026-11-07-persistence.html", :title "Scittlecastr: All You Need is Persistence", :link "https://jmglov.net/blog/2026-11-07-persistence.html", :published-at #inst "2026-11-07T23:59:59.000-00:00", :content "This post was entirely written by a human, with no assistance from LLMs or other \"AI\" technology such as pervert glasses or autonomous weapon systems. Last time on Scittlecastr: The Scittling, we scittled up a podcast player, using the awesome power of the ClojureScript REPL. What we found great about this approach was the ability to create HTML elements on the fly and update the page without having to reload, but at the end of our session, we were struck by the fear that if we do reload, we will lose all the state of our running program. Gritting our teeth, we test our hypothesis... 😭😭😭 The Buddha spake wisely when he claimed that life is a vale of tears. However, we have achieved satori through Scittle, so we discard our fragile ego and expand our consciousness to encompass all things, and discover wisdom in the infinite. This wisdom was that of having done our interactive development in a Rich comment, thus allowing us build our interface back up by evaluating our player.cljs buffer, then stepping through the Rich comment, evaluating each form one by one (you can use cider-eval-last-sexp (C-c C-e) for this, since you don&apost need the comment inserted again). Let&aposs rebuild our initial ugly player, using our slick new functions. Make sure to evaluate the entire buffer after a page reload (C-c C-k), or you&aposll get an error similar to this the first time you try to evaluate a form in the REPL: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (get-el \"body\")\n  ;; => \"#error {:message \\\"Unable to resolve symbol: get-el\\\",\n  ;;             :data {:type :sci/error, :line 1, :column 1, ...}}\n\n  )\n Having evaluated the buffer, let&aposs get ugly! (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (set-children! \"body\"\n                 [(create-el {:tag \"h1\"\n                              :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                              :text \"cljcastr player\"})\n                  (create-el {:tag \"audio\"\n                              :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                              :attrs {:controls true\n                                      :src \"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/5d9048546bacd9001de88f1c/2abc6f33-6a0a-4658-a8e0-c811984c13a0.mp3\"}})])\n  ;; => #object[HTMLBodyElement [object HTMLBodyElement]]\n\n  )\n That is quite a pain, though. Is there a way to effectively export the running program as HTML so we could save it to our index.html? Well, let&aposs give it a go. What we&aposd like is to turn the body DOM node into an HTML fragment. We can actually already do this in a clunky way. We log the element: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (get-el \"body\")\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLBodyElement [object HTMLBodyElement]]\n\n  )\n and then look at the console: Unfortunately, we can&apost directly use the console output in the REPL. We could of course just copy and paste it from the console window, but that feels too efficient, and we&aposd rather spend some time shaving those yaks, so let&aposs think about how we might generate our own HTML from the DOM tree anchored at the body element. First, we need to do a depth-first traversal of the children of body. This is quite easy for the time being, since body only has children and no grandchildren or great-grandchildren or whatever. Let&aposs try to count those children: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def children (-> (get-children \"body\")\n                    log))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/children\n\n  (count children)\n  ;; => 3\n\n  )\n Wait, what&aposs going on here? The JS console actually holds the key. If we look in the console at the previously logged line and expand it, we see:   ▼ (2) [h1.cljcastr, audio.cljcastr, protonpass-root-64f9] ℹ        nrepl.cljs:10\n    ‣ 0: h1.cljcastr\n    ‣ 1: audio.cljcastr\n    ‣ 2: protonpass-root-64f9\n      length: 2\n    ‣ [[Prototype]]: Array(0)\n Looks like my Proton Pass extension is adding an element to the body. 😬 But luckily, we added the cljcastr class to all our elements, so we can use a CSS selector to limit the children to the ones we actually put there ourselves: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def children (-> (js/document.querySelectorAll \"body > .cljcastr\")\n                    seq\n                    log))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/children\n\n  (count children)\n  ;; => 2\n\n  )\n Excellent! Now let&aposs see what the element names of the children are: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> children\n       (map get-name)\n       log)\n  ;; => (\"h1\" \"audio\")\n\n  )\n And their attributes? (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (->> children\n       (map #(.-attributes %))\n       log)\n  ;; => (#object[NamedNodeMap [object NamedNodeMap]]\n  ;;     #object[NamedNodeMap [object NamedNodeMap]])\n\n  )\n Ah yes, thank you for not using a POJSO (Plain &aposOl JavaScript Object, ofc) there. I guess we&aposll need some sort of function to turn a NamedNodeMap into a Clojure map. No matter, we can write some simple Clojure to fix this: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (->> children\n       (map (fn [el]\n              (let [attrs (.-attributes el)]\n                (->> (range (.-length attrs))\n                     (map #(let [attr (.item attrs %)]\n                             [(-> (.-name attr) keyword) (.-value attr)]))\n                     (into {})))))\n       log)\n  ;; => ({:class \"cljcastr\"}\n  ;;     {:controls \"true\",\n  ;;      :src \"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/...\",\n  ;;      :class \"cljcastr\"})\n\n  )\n Um. This is... yeah. I think it&aposs time to write a few functions to decomplect this mess. Let&aposs head back up to the top of the file and add a few little functions: (ns cljcastr.player)\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn get-attr ...)\n\n(defn attr->clj\n  \"Given an entry in an attribute map (i.e. `Attr` in a `NamedNodeMap` that is\n   the value of the `.-attributes` property of an element), returns a vector of\n   the name (as a keyword) and value of the attribute.\"\n  [attr]\n  [(-> (.-name attr) keyword) (.-value attr)])\n\n(defn get-attrs\n  \"Returns the attributes of an element as a map of attribute names (as keywords)\n   to attribute values.\"\n  [el]\n  (let [el (get-el el)\n        attrs (.-attributes el)]\n    (->> (range (.-length attrs))\n         (map #(->> % (.item attrs) attr->clj))\n         (into {}))))\n\n;; (defn set-attr! ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> children\n       (map get-attrs)\n       log)\n  ;; => ({:class \"cljcastr\"}\n  ;;     {:controls \"true\",\n  ;;      :src \"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/...\",\n  ;;      :class \"cljcastr\"})\n\n  )\n Now that we know how to get both the name of the tag and its attributes, let&aposs combine them into a function that we can use to map over a list of elements and transform them into a nice Clojurey data structure. Climbing into our hammock ever so briefly, we sketch out the following: {:tag \"{{tag-name}}\"\n :attrs {:k1 \"{{v1}}\"\n         :k2 \"{{v2}}\"}}\n Given this data structure and the functions we&aposve already written, it&aposs not too difficult to write the code: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (->> children\n       (map (fn [el]\n              {:tag (get-name el)\n               :attrs (get-attrs el)}))\n       log)\n  ;; => ({:tag \"h1\", :attrs {:class \"cljcastr\"}}\n  ;;     {:tag \"audio\",\n  ;;      :attrs {:controls \"true\",\n  ;;              :src \"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/...\",\n  ;;              :class \"cljcastr\"}})\n\n  )\n The only thing missing from this is the inner text for the h1 element. Let&aposs add a :text key to our map for elements that have text: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (->> children\n       (map (fn [el]\n              (let [text (get-text el)]\n                (merge {:tag (get-name el)\n                        :attrs (get-attrs el)}\n                       (when-not (empty? text)\n                         {:text text})))))\n       log)\n  ;; => ({:tag \"h1\", :attrs {:class \"cljcastr\"}, :text \"cljcastr player\"}\n  ;;     {:tag \"audio\",\n  ;;      :attrs {:controls \"true\",\n  ;;              :src \"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/...\",\n  ;;              :class \"cljcastr\"}})\n\n  )\n Let&aposs turn this into a nice function, and whilst we&aposre at it, add all of the keys that create-el takes. We also want to handle the case where the function is called with a Clojure representation of an element (i.e. a map), in which case it should just return the map: (ns cljcastr.player)\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn add-style! ...)\n\n(defn el->clj\n  \"Given an HTML element, returns a map containing :tag, :id, :classes, :styles,\n   :text, and :attrs.\"\n  [el]\n  (if (map? el)\n    el\n    (let [el (get-el el)]\n      (->> {:tag (get-name el)\n            :id (get-id el)\n            :classes (get-classes el)\n            :styles (get-styles el)\n            :text (get-text el)\n            :attrs (get-attrs el)}\n           (remove (fn [[_ v]] (empty? v)))\n           (into {})))))\n\n;; (defn create-el ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (->> children\n       (map el->clj)\n       log)\n  ;; => ({:tag \"h1\",\n  ;;      :classes (\"cljcastr\"),\n  ;;      :text \"cljcastr player\",\n  ;;      :attrs {:class \"cljcastr\"}}\n  ;;     {:tag \"audio\",\n  ;;      :classes (\"cljcastr\"),\n  ;;      :attrs {:controls \"true\",\n  ;;              :src \"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/...\",\n  ;;              :class \"cljcastr\"}})\n\n  )\n I wonder if we can roundtrip an element to a map back to an element? (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> (create-el {:tag \"button\"\n                   :id \"button-play\"\n                   :classes [\"cljcastr\" \"playback-button\"]\n                   :styles {:padding \"0px\"\n                            :margin \"0px\"\n                            :border \"0px\"\n                            :background \"transparent\"\n                            :cursor \"pointer\"}\n                   :attrs {:aria-label \"Play\"}})\n       el->clj\n       create-el\n       log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n\n  )\n Looking good! From this, it&aposs pretty straightforward to turn this into an HTML string: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (->> children\n       (map el->clj)\n       (map (fn [{:keys [tag attrs text]}]\n              (let [attrs-str (if (empty? attrs)\n                                \"\"\n                                (str \" \"\n                                     (->> attrs\n                                          (map (fn [[k v]] (str (name k) \"=\\\"\" v \"\\\"\")))\n                                          (str/join \" \"))))]\n                (str \"<\" tag attrs-str \">\" text \"</\" tag \">\"))))\n       (str/join \"\\n\")\n       log)\n  ;; => \"<h1 class=\\\"cljcastr\\\">cljcastr player</h1>\\n\n  ;;     <audio controls=\\\"true\\\"\n  ;;       src=\\\"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/...\\\"\n  ;;       class=\\\"cljcastr\\\"></audio>\"\n\n  )\n That looks pretty reasonable, so let&aposs extract a few functions: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn el->clj ...)\n\n(defn attrs->str\n  \"Given a map of attributes, returns a string of k=\\\"v\\\"...\"\n  [attrs]\n  (->> attrs\n       (map (fn [[k v]] (str (name k) \"=\\\"\" v \"\\\"\")))\n       (str/join \" \")))\n\n(defn el->html\n  \"Given an element (or the Clojure representation of an element), returns an\n   HTML string.\"\n  [el]\n  (let [{:keys [tag attrs text]} (if (map? el) el (el->clj el))\n        attrs-str (attrs->str attrs)]\n    (str \"<\" tag (if (empty? attrs-str) \"\" (str \" \" attrs-str)) \">\"\n         text\n         \"</\" tag \">\")))\n\n;; (defn create-el ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (->> children\n       (map el->html)\n       (str/join \"\\n\")\n       log)\n  ;; => \"<h1 class=\\\"cljcastr\\\">cljcastr player</h1>\\n\n  ;;     <audio controls=\\\"true\\\"\n  ;;       src=\\\"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/...\\\"\n  ;;       class=\\\"cljcastr\\\"></audio>\"\n\n  )\n Just as we&aposre about to paste our HTML into index.html, a thought occurs to us. We wrote some mucky DOM-tacular JS-interop code to create elements, but now we have an elegant Clojure data structure to describe our elements, and we can turn that into HTML, so why not into DOM nodes as well? Well, given the way we&aposve built up our functions for dealing with the DOM, it turns out we can do this easily! (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (create-el {:tag \"h1\", :attrs {:class \"cljcastr\"}, :text \"cljcastr player\"})\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLHeadingElement [object HTMLHeadingElement]]\n  \n  )\n Looking at the browser console, we&aposre convinced we&aposre onto something:   <h1 class=\"cljcastr\">cljcastr player</h1>                          nrepl.cljs:10\n Looks great! Now that we have a nicer way to build up elements interactively, as well as a way to dehydrate elements into HTML, let&aposs think about what it would take to dehydrate the entire DOM tree rooted at our body tag. Exporting HTML We pretty much have everything we need: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> (get-els \"body > .cljcastr\")\n       (map el->html)\n       (str/join \"\\n\")\n       log)\n  ;; => \"<h1 class=\\\"cljcastr\\\">cljcastr player</h1>\\n\n  ;;     <audio controls=\\\"true\\\"\n  ;;       src=\\\"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/...\\\"\n  ;;       class=\\\"cljcastr\\\"></audio>\"\n\n  )\n This looks great, so let&aposs build the full podcast player we had before back up in our REPL and try dehydrating that into HTML. Might as well reload the page to make sure we&aposre starting out with a pristine REPL. Remember to eval the buffer (C-c C-k) after reloading! To rebuild our player, we could just step through the entire Rich comment from the beginning, evaluating each form one by one, but this sounds like a lot of work! Let&aposs add a new add-attrs! function and another adornment to our create-el function to make life easier on ourselves: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn set-attrs! ...)\n;; ...\n\n(defn add-attrs! [el attrs]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (set-attrs! el (merge (get-attrs el) attrs))))\n\n;; (defn get-id ...)\n;; ...\n;; (defn el->html ...)\n\n(defn create-el [{:keys [tag id classes styles text attrs children] :as args}]\n  (-> (js/document.createElement tag)\n      (set-attrs! attrs)\n      (set-id! id)\n      (set-classes! classes)\n      (set-styles! styles)\n      (set-children! children)\n      (set-text! text)))\n\n;; (defn add-event-listener! ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                   :id \"episode-container\"\n                   :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                   :styles {:width \"100%\"\n                            :display \"flex\"\n                            :flex-direction \"column\"\n                            :align-items \"center\"}\n                   :children\n                   [\n                    ;; episode-info\n                    (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                                :id \"episode-info\"\n                                :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                :styles {:max-width \"420px\"\n                                         :text-align \"center\"}\n                                :children\n                                [(create-el {:tag \"h3\"\n                                             :id \"podcast-title\"\n                                             :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                             :text \"defn\"})\n                                 (create-el {:tag \"h2\"\n                                             :id \"episode-title\"\n                                             :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                             :text \"876b4c306172 Cora Sutton\"})\n                                 (create-el {:tag \"span\"\n                                             :id \"episode-date\"\n                                             :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                             :styles {:color \"grey\"}\n                                             :text \"July 9, 2025\"})]})\n\n                    ;;episode-art\n                    (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                                :id \"episode-art-container\"\n                                :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                :children\n                                [(create-el {:tag \"img\"\n                                             :id \"episode-art\"\n                                             :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                             :styles {:width \"400px\"\n                                                      :border-radius \"5%\"\n                                                      :padding \"10px\"}\n                                             :attrs {:src \"https://media.zencastr.com/image-files/5d9048546bacd9001de88f1c/a5b922ac-7294-4d58-9a33-4308f5d4f50b.png\"}})]})\n\n                    ;;audio\n                    (-> (create-el {:tag \"audio\"\n                                    :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                    :attrs {:controls true\n                                            :src \"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/5d9048546bacd9001de88f1c/2abc6f33-6a0a-4658-a8e0-c811984c13a0.mp3\"}\n                                    :styles {:display \"none\"}})\n                        (add-event-listener! \"timeupdate\"\n                                             (fn [ev]\n                                               (let [audio (.-target ev)]\n                                                 (update-playback-position-indicators!\n                                                  (.-currentTime audio) (.-duration audio)\n                                                  {:elapsed-time \"#elapsed-time\"\n                                                   :remaining-time \"#remaining-time\"\n                                                   :progress-slider \"#progress-slider\"\n                                                   :progress-bar \"#progress-bar\"})))))\n\n                    ;;progress\n                    (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                                :id \"progress\"\n                                :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                :styles {:width \"390px\"\n                                         :margin \"1.5em auto 0 auto\"}\n                                :children\n                                [(create-el {:tag \"span\"\n                                             :text (secs->ts 0)\n                                             :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                             :id \"elapsed-time\"\n                                             :styles {:margin \"0 5px 0 5px\"\n                                                      :font-size \"0.7rem\"\n                                                      :color \"grey\"\n                                                      :float \"left\"}})\n                                 (create-el {:tag \"span\"\n                                             :text (secs->ts 0)\n                                             :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                             :id \"remaining-time\"\n                                             :styles {:margin \"0 5px 0 5px\"\n                                                      :font-size \"0.7rem\"\n                                                      :color \"grey\"\n                                                      :float \"right\"}})\n                                 (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                                             :id \"progress-container\"\n                                             :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                             :styles {:position \"relative\"\n                                                      :height \"28px\"\n                                                      :clear \"both\"}\n                                             :children\n                                             [(create-el {:tag \"progress\"\n                                                          :id \"progress-bar\"\n                                                          :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                                          :attrs {:min 0\n                                                                  :max 0\n                                                                  :value 0}\n                                                          :styles {:appearance \"none\"\n                                                                   :height \"8px\"\n                                                                   :width \"100%\"\n                                                                   :border \"0\"\n                                                                   :border-radius \"4px\"\n                                                                   :margin \"0\"\n                                                                   :overflow \"hidden\"\n                                                                   :background-color \"lightgray\"\n                                                                   :color \"#f7931e\"\n                                                                   :position \"absolute\"\n                                                                   :top \"10px\"\n                                                                   :left \"0px\"\n                                                                   :z-index 0}})\n                                              (create-el {:tag \"input\"\n                                                          :id \"progress-slider\"\n                                                          :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                                          :attrs {:type \"range\"\n                                                                  :min 0\n                                                                  :max 0\n                                                                  :value 0\n                                                                  :step \"any\"\n                                                                  :aria-label \"Seek\"\n                                                                  :aria-valuetext (str (secs->ts 0)\n                                                                                       \" of \"\n                                                                                       (secs->ts 0))\n                                                                  :aria-valuemax 0}\n                                                          :styles {:appearance \"none\"\n                                                                   :cursor \"pointer\"\n                                                                   :height \"28px\"\n                                                                   :width \"100%\"\n                                                                   :border \"0\"\n                                                                   :margin \"0\"\n                                                                   :touch-action \"manipulation\"\n                                                                   :position \"absolute\"\n                                                                   :top \"0px\"\n                                                                   :left \"0px\"\n                                                                   :z-index 1\n                                                                   :background-color \"transparent\"}})]})]})\n\n                    ;;playback-controls\n                    (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                                :id \"playback-controls\"\n                                :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                :children\n                                (->> [:backward :play :pause :forward :speed]\n                                     (map (fn [button-name]\n                                            [button-name (get playback-controls button-name)]))\n                                     (map (fn [[button-name button]]\n                                            (let [button-el (create-button button-name button\n                                                                           {:seek-dur-sec seek-dur-sec\n                                                                            :button-class \"playback-button\"\n                                                                            :img-class \"playback-icon\"})]\n                                              (-> (get-children button-el)\n                                                  first\n                                                  (set-styles! {:width \"35px\"\n                                                                :filter (str \"invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%) \"\n                                                                             \"hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%)\")}))\n                                              button-el))) log)})]})\n       log\n       list\n       (set-children! \"body\"))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLBodyElement [object HTMLBodyElement]]\n\n  (let [audio (get-el \"audio.cljcastr\")]\n    (set-text! \"#elapsed-time\" (secs->ts (.-currentTime audio)))\n    (set-text! \"#remaining-time\" (secs->ts (- (.-duration audio)\n                                              (.-currentTime audio))))\n    (add-attrs! \"#progress-bar\" {:max (.-duration audio)\n                                 :value (.-currentTime audio)})\n    (add-attrs! \"#progress-slider\" {:max (.-duration audio)\n                                    :value (.-currentTime audio)\n                                    :aria-valuetext (str (secs->ts (.-currentTime audio))\n                                                         \" of \"\n                                                         (secs->ts (.-duration audio)))\n                                    :aria-valuemax (.-duration audio)}))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLInputElement [object HTMLInputElement]]\n  \n  )\n Much like Lazarus, our podcast player hath now been resurrected! And also like Lazarus, the method of resurrection required divine powers. But fear not! for this shall be the last time we rely on the devine to rebuild our player. Let&aposs get back to dehydration, shall we? We can use the same method as before, when we had a really simple player: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> (get-els \"body > .cljcastr\")\n       (map el->html)\n       (str/join \"\\n\")\n       log)\n  ;; => \"<div id=\\\"episode-container\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\"\n  ;;       style=\\\"width: 100%; display: flex; flex-direction: column;\n  ;;       align-items: center;\\\">\n  ;;       defn876b4c306172 Cora SuttonJuly 9, 202500:001:29:30</div>\"\n\n  )\n Hrm, that&aposs not very helpful. First of all, we&aposre only getting the direct children of our body element, which in this case is a single child: the episode-container div. What we&aposd actually like is the entire tree of elements rooted at body. Let&aposs see if we can figure out how to do that. (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> (get-els \"body > .cljcastr\")\n       (map (fn [el]\n              (let [{:keys [tag attrs text]} (if (map? el) el (el->clj el))\n                    attrs-str (attrs->str attrs)\n                    tag-open (str \"  <\" tag (if (empty? attrs-str) \"\" (str \" \" attrs-str)) \">\")\n                    tag-close (str \"</\" tag \">\")\n                    children (get-children el)]\n                (if (seq children)\n                  (str/join \"\\n\"\n                            (concat [tag-open]\n                                    (map el->html children)\n                                    [tag-close]))\n                  (str tag-open text tag-close)))))\n       (str/join \"\\n\")\n       log)\n  ;; => \"<div id=\\\"episode-container\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\" style=\\\"...\\\">\\n\n  ;;       <div id=\\\"episode-info\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\" style=\\\"...\\\">defn876b4c306172 Cora SuttonJuly 9, 2025</div>\\n\n  ;;       <div id=\\\"episode-art-container\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\"></div>\\n\n  ;;       <audio controls=\\\"true\\\" src=\\\"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/...\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\" style=\\\"display: none;\\\"></audio>\\n\n  ;;       <div id=\\\"progress\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\" style=\\\"...\\\">00:37-1:28:53</div>\\n\n  ;;       <div id=\\\"playback-controls\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\"></div>\\n\n  ;;       </div>\"\n\n  )\n OK, this is progress, but we&aposre only grabbing the first level of children. In order to include grandchildren and great-grandchildren and great-great-grandchildren (and so on to the last syllable of recorded time), we&aposll need to include our new code in el->html itself: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn attrs->str ...)\n\n(defn el->html\n  \"Given an element (or the Clojure representation of an element), returns an\n   HTML string.\"\n  [el]\n  (let [{:keys [tag attrs text]} (if (map? el) el (el->clj el))\n        attrs-str (attrs->str attrs)\n        tag-open (str \"  <\" tag (if (empty? attrs-str) \"\" (str \" \" attrs-str)) \">\")\n        tag-close (str \"</\" tag \">\")\n        children (get-children el)]\n    (if (seq children)\n      (str/join \"\\n\"\n                (concat [tag-open]\n                        (map el->html children)\n                        [tag-close]))\n      (str tag-open text tag-close))))\n\n;; (defn create-el ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> (get-els \"body > .cljcastr\")\n       (map el->html)\n       (str/join \"\\n\")\n       log)\n  ;; => \"#error {:message \\\"Failed to execute &aposquerySelector&apos on &aposDocument&apos:\n  ;;                        &apos[object Text]&apos is not a valid selector.\\\",\n  ;;             ...}\"\n\n  )\n Well, that doesn&apost look promising. Let&aposs see if we can figure this out. Looking back out at the HTML we generated in previously, we get a hint about which element might be the culprit:   ;; => \"<div id=\\\"episode-container\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\" style=\\\"...\\\">\\n\n  ;;       <div id=\\\"episode-info\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\" style=\\\"...\\\">defn876b4c306172 Cora SuttonJuly 9, 2025</div>\\n\n Our #episode-info div looks a bit odd, doesn&apost it. Let&aposs take a closer look: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (get-children \"#episode-info\")\n  ;; => (#object[HTMLHeadingElement [object HTMLHeadingElement]]\n  ;;     #object[HTMLHeadingElement [object HTMLHeadingElement]]\n  ;;     #object[HTMLSpanElement [object HTMLSpanElement]])\n\n  )\n OK, so #episode-info has three children: two headings and a span. Let&aposs feed the first child to el->html and see what happens: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (get-children \"#episode-info\")\n      first\n      el->html)\n  ;; => \"#error {:message \\\"Failed to execute &aposquerySelector&apos on &aposDocument&apos:\n  ;;                        &apos[object Text]&apos is not a valid selector.\\\",\n  ;;             ...}\"\n\n  (-> (get-children \"#episode-info\")\n      first\n      get-children\n      log)\n  ;; => (#object[Text [object Text]])\n\n  )\n Ah yes, there it is. So there seems to be something special about a Text node that causes el->html to blow up. Let&aposs see what we can actually do with this type of object. Reading the documentation, we see that it inherits from the CharacterData class, which has a handy data attribute that is \"string representing the textual data contained in this object\". Let&aposs try that out: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (get-children \"#episode-info\")\n      first\n      get-children\n      first\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[Text [object Text]]\n\n  (-> (get-children \"#episode-info\")\n      first\n      get-children\n      first\n      (.-data))\n  ;; => \"defn\"\n\n  )\n Armed with this knowledge, we should be able to fix get-text to handle Text nodes: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn get-name ...)\n\n(defn get-text\n  \"Returns the inner text of an element.\"\n  [el]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (if (instance? js/CharacterData el)\n      (.-data el)\n      (or (.-textContent el)\n          (.-innerText el)))))\n\n;; (defn set-text! ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (get-children \"#episode-info\")\n      first\n      get-children\n      first\n      get-text)\n  ;; => \"#error {:message \\\"Failed to execute &aposquerySelector&apos on &aposDocument&apos:\n  ;;                        &apos[object Text]&apos is not a valid selector.\\\",\n  ;;             ...}\"\n\n  )\n Hrm, we&aposre back to this. Let&aposs trace through get-text line by line to see if we can figure out what&aposs going on: (let [el (get-el el)]\n  ...)\n So we&aposd be calling (get-el) with our Text node. Let&aposs try that and see what happens: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (get-children \"#episode-info\")\n      first\n      get-children\n      first\n      get-el)\n  ;; => \"#error {:message \\\"Failed to execute &aposquerySelector&apos on &aposDocument&apos:\n  ;;                        &apos[object Text]&apos is not a valid selector.\\\",\n  ;;             ...}\"\n\n  )\n Aha, so the problem is in get-el, which looks like this: (defn get-el [selector]\n  (if (instance? js/Element selector)\n    selector\n    (js/document.querySelector selector)))\n What we&aposre trying to do here is just return the selector arg if we&aposre passed an element, otherwise assume selector is a string and use Document.querySelector to look up the element. But wait, why should we assume it&aposs a string? Why not actually check? In fact, why not actually write the function in the same way we would describe it in English? If selector is a string, returns the element matching CSS selector. Otherwise, assume it&aposs already an element and just return it. And whilst we&aposre at it, why not update the docstring of the function to say exactly this? (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn secs->ts ...)\n\n(defn get-el\n  \"If `selector` is a string, returns the element matching CSS selector.\n   Otherwise, assume it&aposs already an element and just return it.\"\n  [selector]\n  (if (string? selector)\n    (js/document.querySelector selector)\n    selector))\n\n;; (defn get-els ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (get-children \"#episode-info\")\n      first\n      get-children\n      first\n      get-text)\n  ;; => \"defn\"\n\n  )\n Much better! However, we still have that annoying word \"assume\" in our docstring. 😬 Returns the element matching the specified CSS selector. If selector is already an element, just returns the element. How about we stop assuming, and actually throw an exception when we get an argument we can&apost handle? Sounds good, but the reason we&aposre in this mess is because we were treating anything that wasn&apost an Element object as a string, and we discovered that Text isn&apost a subclass of Element. So what we actually want is a common ancestor of both Text and Element, right? And luckily, there is just such a subclass: Node! 🎉 Let&aposs use that: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn secs->ts ...)\n\n(defn get-el\n  \"If `selector` is a string, returns the element matching CSS selector.\n   If `selector` is a `Node`, returns it. Otherwise, throws an exception.\"\n  [selector]\n  (cond\n    (string? selector)\n    (js/document.querySelector selector)\n\n    (instance? js/Node selector)\n    selector\n\n    :else\n    (let [cls (-> selector .-constructor .-name)]\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"Expecting a string or a Node; got: \" cls)\n                      {:dom/error :dom/error.invalid-class\n                       :js/class cls})))))\n\n;; (defn get-els ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (get-el \"#podcast-title\")\n  ;; => #object[HTMLHeadingElement [object HTMLHeadingElement]]\n\n  (get-el (js/document.querySelector \"#podcast-title\"))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLHeadingElement [object HTMLHeadingElement]]\n\n  (get-el 42)\n  ;; => \"#error {:message \\\"Expecting a string or a Node; got: Number\\\", ...}\"\n\n  (try\n    (get-el 42)\n    (catch js/Error e\n      (ex-data e)))\n  ;; => {:dom/error :dom/error.invalid-class, :js/class \"Number\"}\n\n  (-> (get-children \"#episode-info\")\n      first\n      get-children\n      first\n      get-text)\n  ;; => \"defn\"\n\n  )\n Let&aposs try turning the #episode-info div into HTML: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (el->html \"#episode-info\")\n  ;; => \"#error {:message \\\"Could not find instance method: getAttribute\\\", ...}\"\n\n  )\n Let&aposs slow down for a second and actually think this through. What we&aposre dealing with is HTML like this: <h3>defn</h3>\n which is represented by a DOM node that we can create like this: (create-el {:tag \"h3\", :text \"defn\"})\n And if we currently try to turn this into HTML, we get the \"Could not find instance method: getAttribute\" error, which makes sense, because neither Text nor any of its parent classes (CharacterData and Node) have a .getAttribute() method, and el->html calls el->clj, which in turn will try to get all sorts of attributes from the poor Text object. 😭 We should be able to solve this by making sure el->html can handle a Text node: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn attrs->str ...)\n\n(defn el->html\n  \"Given an element (or the Clojure representation of an element), returns an\n   HTML string.\"\n  [el]\n  (if (instance? js/CharacterData el)\n    (get-text el)\n    (let [{:keys [tag attrs text]} (if (map? el) el (el->clj el))\n          attrs-str (attrs->str attrs)\n          tag-open (str \"  <\" tag (if (empty? attrs-str) \"\" (str \" \" attrs-str)) \">\")\n          tag-close (str \"</\" tag \">\")\n          children (get-children el)]\n      (if (seq children)\n        (str/join \"\\n\"\n                  (concat [tag-open]\n                          (map el->html children)\n                          [tag-close]))\n        (str tag-open text tag-close)))))\n\n;; (defn format-css ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (el->html (js/Text. \"foo\"))\n  ;; => \"foo\"\n\n  (-> (create-el {:tag \"h3\", :text \"defn\"})\n      el->html)\n  ;; => \"  <h3>\\ndefn\\n</h3>\"\n\n  )\n That seems somewhat reasonable, so let&aposs see what happens when we turn the entire #episode-info div into HTML now: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (el->html \"#episode-info\")\n  ;; => \"  <div id=\\\"episode-info\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\" style=\\\"max-width: 420px; text-align: center;\\\">\\n\n  ;;       <h3 id=\\\"podcast-title\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\">\\ndefn\\n</h3>\\n\n  ;;       <h2 id=\\\"episode-title\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\">\\n876b4c306172 Cora Sutton\\n</h2>\\n\n  ;;       <span id=\\\"episode-date\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\" style=\\\"color: grey;\\\">\\nJuly 9, 2025\\n</span>\\n\n  ;;       </div>\"\n\n  )\n Hey, not bad! Feeling confident, we go ahead and dump the entire DOM to HTML: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (el->html \"html\")\n      log)\n  ;; => \"  <html xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\\\">\\n\n  ;;       <head>\\n\\n\n  ;;       \\n\n  ;;       <title>\\ncljcastr episode player\\n</title>\\n\\n\n  ;;       \\n\n  ;;       <link rel=\\\"stylesheet\\\" href=\\\"main.css\\\"></link>\\n\\n\\n\n  ;;       \\n\n  ;;       <script src=\\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/scittle@0.8.33/dist/scittle.js\\\" type=\\\"application/javascript\\\"></script>\\n\\n\n  ;;       \\n\n  ;;       <script>\\nvar SCITTLE_NREPL_WEBSOCKET_PORT = 1340;\\n</script>\\n\\n\n  ;;       \\n\n  ;;       <script src=\\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/scittle@0.8.33/dist/scittle.nrepl.js\\\" type=\\\"application/javascript\\\"></script>\\n\\n\\n\\n\n  ;;       <script type=\\\"application/x-scittle\\\" src=\\\"player.cljs\\\"></script>\\n\\n\\n</head>\\n\\n\\n\\n\n  ;;       <body>\\n\n  ;;       <div class=\\\"translate-tooltip-mtz blue sm-root translate hidden_translate\\\">\n  ;;       <div class=\\\"header-wrapper\\\">\n  ;;       <div class=\\\"header-controls\\\">\n  ;;       ...\n  ;;       </div>\n  ;;       <span class=\\\"translate-button-mtz hidden_translate blue\\\"></span>\\n\n  ;;       <audio class=\\\"audio-for-speech\\\" src=\\\"\\\"></audio>\\n\n  ;;       <div id=\\\"episode-container\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\" style=\\\"width: 100%; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;\\\">\\n\n  ;;       <div id=\\\"episode-info\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\" style=\\\"max-width: 420px; text-align: center;\\\">\\n\n  ;;       <h3 id=\\\"podcast-title\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\">\\ndefn\\n</h3>\\n\n  ;;       <h2 id=\\\"episode-title\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\">\\n876b4c306172 Cora Sutton\\n</h2>\\n\n  ;;       <span id=\\\"episode-date\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr\\\" style=\\\"color: grey;\\\">\\nJuly 9, 2025\\n</span>\\n\n  ;;       </div>\\n\n  ;;       ...\n  ;;       <button aria-label=\\\"Playback speed\\\" id=\\\"button-speed\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr playback-button\\\" style=\\\"padding: 0px; margin: 0px; border: 0px; background: transparent; cursor: pointer;\\\">\\n\n  ;;       <img src=\\\"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/151785/speed-meter.svg\\\" draggable=\\\"false\\\" id=\\\"button-speed-icon\\\" class=\\\"cljcastr playback-icon\\\" style=\\\"width: 35px; filter: invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%) hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%);\\\"></img>\\n\n  ;;       </button>\\n\n  ;;       </div>\\n\n  ;;       </div>\\n\n  ;;       </body>\\n\n  ;;       </html>\"\n Now that&aposs more like it! Better yet, if we look in the browser console, we see some HTML: Let&aposs copy this HTML from the console and paste it into our index.html:   <html xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">\n  <head>\n\n  \n  <title>\ncljcastr episode player\n</title>\n\n  \n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"main.css\"></link>\n\n\n  \n  <script src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/scittle@0.8.33/dist/scittle.js\" type=\"application/javascript\"></script>\n\n  \n  <script>\nvar SCITTLE_NREPL_WEBSOCKET_PORT = 1340;\n</script>\n\n  \n  <script src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/scittle@0.8.33/dist/scittle.nrepl.js\" type=\"application/javascript\"></